"""Dubrovnik Old Town walking tour for Kim and Dave Williams (cruise stop, Monday 12 October 2026).

Narration text as it is sent to ElevenLabs. Source: the handoff "Dubrovnik Audio Scripts.md" (8 tracks), with
Radar's fact-check corrections of 11 October 2026:
- Fort Lovrijenac: walls about 12 m (nearly 40 ft) toward the sea, about 60 cm toward the city (was "twelve feet").
- Quarantine: Dubrovnik's 1377 law used 30 days; the word comes from the later 40-day rule (Italian "quaranta").
- Dropped "Libertas carved over the sea gate" (not confirmed); kept the flag and the Lovrijenac inscription.
- Rector: dropped "no key to the treasury" (not confirmed). Asclepius explained by the Epidaurum/Cavtat claim,
  not by "justice as healing" (invented). 1463 second explosion and Michelozzo put in order. Pracat: the only
  citizen the Republic honored with a statue.
- Cathedral treasury: skull, arms and a leg of St Blaise (sources), not "hands and a foot".
- Gundulic: the liberty hymn is from Dubravka; the 50-kuna note is from before the 2023 switch to the euro.
  Dropped the unconfirmed paving and market-date details.
- St Blaise statue: "one of the best pictures" of the pre-1667 city, not "the only".
Croatian names are respelled phonetically in the spoken text only. <break> tags are ElevenLabs pauses.
"""

P = '<break time="1.5s" />'
LP = '<break time="3.0s" />'

TRACKS = [
("00-Pile-Gate", "Pile Gate", "Welcome to Ragusa", f"""
Stop for a second. Feel that? The stone under your feet is cool, the air smells like sea salt and warm limestone, and in front of you is a wall nearly two kilometers around that has been standing guard for centuries.

Kim, Dave, welcome to Dubrovnik. {P} Or, as the people who built it would have said, welcome to Ragusa, one of the most stubbornly independent little countries in the history of the Mediterranean.

Here's the story in one breath. For more than four hundred years, from 1358 until Napoleon's army marched in in 1808, this city was its own republic. No king. No emperor. It survived by being clever, by being rich, and by being very, very good at diplomacy. It paid tribute to the Ottomans, it kept Venice at arm's length, and it never stopped smiling while doing it. {P}

Its motto was a single word: Libertas. Liberty. It flew on the Republic's flag. And look for the big fortress on the cliff above the sea, behind you and off to your right. That's Fort Lov-ree-YEH-nats. Over its entrance is a famous Latin line that says, roughly: "Liberty is not sold for all the gold in the world." {P}

That fortress has a trick. The walls facing the sea are nearly forty feet thick. The wall facing the city is barely two feet thick. That was deliberate. If a commander inside ever turned against the Republic, the city's own cannons could knock that thin wall down and take the fort back. Clever little country. {LP}

Now look at the gate itself. Over the arch is a stone statue of Saint Blaise, the city's patron saint. You'll meet him a lot today. This outer gate dates from 1537, and in those days the drawbridge you're about to walk across was pulled up every night. Dubrovnik went to bed with the drawbridge up.

This city was also a pioneer of public health. In 1377 it passed a law holding ships and travelers apart for thirty days before letting them in, to keep out the plague. It's one of the first quarantine laws in the world. Later the wait grew to forty days, "quaranta" in Italian, and that gave us the word quarantine. {P} So if you ever feel sorry for a cruise ship stuck in port, just remember: Dubrovnik got there first.

Okay. Here's your job. Walk across the bridge, through the dark inner gate, and when you come out into the light, stop. On your right you'll see a big round fountain with sixteen carved stone faces spouting water. That's Onofrio's Fountain, finished in 1444. Fill a bottle or splash your face. It's been refreshing travelers for nearly six hundred years.

Then look for the church and monastery on your left, just a few steps down the main street. That's our first stop. Press play on track one when you're standing in front of its carved doorway.
"""),

("01-Franciscan-Monastery", "Franciscan Monastery and Church", "A pharmacy since 1317", f"""
You're standing on the Stradun, the main street of Dubrovnik, and I want you to look down at it. See how the stones are polished and shiny, almost like glass? Centuries of feet did that. Yours are now part of the story. {P}

Behind these walls is a monastery that has been here since 1317. That's when the Franciscan brothers moved inside the city walls. Their first home was outside the walls, and the city brought them in for safety.

Three things to know. One: the pharmacy. {P} The monks opened a pharmacy here in 1317, and it is still in business. It's often called the third oldest working pharmacy in the world. Same brotherhood, same spot, seven hundred years. They still sell creams made from old recipes, including a rose cream. It makes a great gift, and if anyone asks where you got it, you can say "from the monks, since 1317."

Two: the doorway. See the stone carving above it? A mother holding her son after the crucifixion. That's a Pietà, carved in 1498. {P} And here's why it matters. In 1667, a huge earthquake hit Dubrovnik. It leveled most of the city, killed thousands of people, and brought down the church behind this door. But this carved doorway survived. It's the only piece of the old church front you can still see.

Three: the cloister. If you buy a ticket and step inside, you'll find one of the loveliest courtyards in Croatia, carved in the 1300s by a master named Mee-hoy-eh Bry-kov, with about a hundred and twenty slender columns standing in pairs. Look closely at the tops of them. Each one is carved differently, with leaves, strange faces and little beasts, each a stonecutter's private joke. {P} Palms and orange trees grow in the middle. It's cool and quiet, the best five minutes of peace you'll find in town.

These walls also remember the siege of 1991, when this monastery was hit by shelling and then lovingly repaired. When you notice a roof in Dubrovnik that's a brighter orange than its neighbors, that's usually a roof replaced after the war. The old city has healed, but it has not forgotten.

Your action: step inside the church, even just for a minute. Then, if the cloister and museum are open, go in. When you come back out onto the Stradun, turn right and stroll to the far end of the street, about three hundred yards, toward the tall bell tower. Take your time. Play track two when you reach the elegant building on your left with the arched porch, just before the tower.
"""),

("02-Sponza-Palace", "Sponza Palace", "Honest scales", f"""
Walk through the arches into the courtyard. Hear how the noise of the street drops away? Congratulations. You're inside the old customs house of the Republic of Ragusa.

This is Sponza Palace. {P} It was built between 1516 and 1522, and it's a lovely mix: Gothic windows upstairs and Renaissance arches down below. It was designed by a local architect named Pas-KOY-eh Mee-LEE-cheh-vich, with carving by a family of stonecutters, the An-DREE-ich brothers. Think of it as a bank vault dressed up as a palace.

The Republic's wealth passed through here. Goods coming into port were weighed, taxed and stored in this courtyard. Over the years the building also served as a mint where coins were made, a treasury, an armory, a bank, and even a school. Imagine silk traders from Venice, spice merchants from the Ottoman lands, and Dubrovnik's own sea captains all bargaining here in half a dozen languages. {P}

Now look up at the arch in the courtyard. There's an inscription in Latin. Here's what it says: "Our weights do not permit cheating. When I measure goods, God measures with me." {P} That's the Republic's business creed, carved in stone. Honest scales, and God is watching.

And here's the best part. When the great earthquake of 1667 flattened Dubrovnik, Sponza Palace came through almost untouched. It's one of the few buildings from before the quake you can still see in its original form. What you're looking at is truly the old Ragusa.

Today the palace holds the state archives, with documents going back to the year 1022. Nearly a thousand years of the city's letters, laws, deeds and wills, kept inside these walls.

Inside you'll also find a memorial room to the defenders of Dubrovnik who died in 1991 and 1992, with their photographs lined up on the wall. Take a quiet moment there if you go in. It's a powerful place, and it's part of why this building matters so much to the city today.

Your action: wander the courtyard, and step into the memorial room if it's open. When you come back outside, you'll be on the little square called Loo-zha. Find the tall stone column with a carved knight on top. Walk toward it, and press play on track three.
"""),

("03-St-Blaise-and-Clock-Tower", "St. Blaise Church and the Clock Tower", "The saint who saved a city", f"""
Loo-zha Square. This is where the Republic of Ragusa did its public business. Announcements, celebrations, punishments, feast days. If something happened in Dubrovnik, it happened right here.

See the tall stone column with the knight in armor? That's Orlando, known elsewhere as Roland, the hero of the old French epic poems. {P} He was carved in 1418, and he stood for the city's freedom. The Republic's flag flew from a pole above him. Now look closely at his forearm. The length from his elbow to his wrist was the city's official measure for cloth, called the Dubrovnik ell, about twenty inches. If you were a merchant, this knight's arm was your ruler.

Now turn to the beautiful church with the grand front steps. That's the Church of Saint Blaise, in Croatian "Sveti VLAH-ho." {P} Blaise was a bishop in Armenia who was killed for his faith in the early three hundreds. And his story here is something special.

In the year 971, the legend goes, Blaise appeared in a vision to a priest in Dubrovnik and warned him that a Venetian fleet, anchored nearby and pretending to be friendly, was really planning to take the city. The city was warned and ready, and the Venetians sailed away with nothing. From that day, Blaise became the guardian of Dubrovnik. {P} His figure is over the gates and on the walls, and his feast day, February third, is still celebrated here with a great procession that UNESCO now recognizes as a treasure of world heritage.

The church you see is Baroque, finished in 1715 by a Venetian architect named Marino Gropelli, after the old church burned in a fire in 1706. One thing survived that fire, and it's inside: a gilded silver statue of Saint Blaise holding a small model of the city in his hand. That model shows Dubrovnik as it looked before the 1667 earthquake. It's one of the best pictures we have of the old city. Step inside and look for it on the main altar. {P}

Finally, look up at the Clock Tower, about a hundred feet tall. A tower has stood here since 1444. Its great bronze bell was cast in 1506, and the tower was rebuilt in 1929 after it started to lean. Look for the two little bronze figures at the top who strike the hours. The locals call them the green men, because their bronze has turned green over the centuries. {LP}

Your action: go into the church for a few minutes. Then look for the pale stone palace with the long arched porch, just along from the church. That's our next stop, the Rector's Palace. Press play on track four when you're standing in front of its arches.
"""),

("04-Rectors-Palace", "Rector's Palace", "One month in charge", f"""
Welcome to the home of the least powerful, most important man in Dubrovnik.

This is the Rector's Palace. {P} For centuries, the Republic was led by a rector who was elected for just one month. One month! And he couldn't be elected again for two years. The idea was that nobody should ever hold power long enough to become a king.

And here's the wild part. During that month, the rector lived in this palace, wore a long red robe, and was not allowed to leave the building except on official business. {P} Imagine being the head of the country and not being allowed to go out to dinner.

Look at the building in front of you. Gothic windows upstairs, a Renaissance porch below. It's a layer cake of centuries. It started as a fortress. Then, in 1435, gunpowder stored inside exploded and wrecked it, and the Republic hired an architect from Naples, Onofrio della Cava, the same man who built the fountain by the gate. In 1463 another explosion hit, and the famous Florentine architect Michelozzo helped with the rebuilding. After the earthquake of 1667 it was repaired once more. The result is a beautiful patchwork.

Now look at the carved tops of the columns on the porch. On one of them is Asclepius, the Greek god of medicine, at work among his jars and potions. {P} Why a god of medicine on a government building? The people of Dubrovnik traced their roots to the ancient town of Epidaurum, just down the coast at today's Tsav-tat, and they proudly claimed Asclepius as one of their own.

Step into the courtyard if the door is open. You'll find a bust of a merchant and sea captain named Miho PRAH-tsat, who left his fortune to the Republic when he died in 1607. He's the only citizen the Republic ever honored with a statue. Everyone else had to settle for a thank-you note. {P}

And above the door to the great council hall is one of my favorite Latin lines in all of Europe. "Obliti privatorum, publica curate." It means: "Forget your private affairs, and take care of the public ones." Imagine if every leader had that sentence over their office door.

A little pop culture: this palace appeared in Game of Thrones, as a grand palace in the city of Qarth. But honestly, the real story is better.

Your action: if you want to go in, the Cultural History Museum inside has beautiful period rooms, and the courtyard is a lovely place to sit. Otherwise, walk a few steps along the palace to the big Baroque church with the dome. That's the Cathedral. Press play on track five when you reach its doors.
"""),

("05-Cathedral", "Dubrovnik Cathedral", "Richard the Lionheart's church", f"""
Look up at the dome. Now look at the stone. It's soft and cream-colored, and it seems lit from within, even on a gray day.

This is the Cathedral of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary. And it begins with a legend that's almost too good to be true. {P}

In the year 1192, the story goes, King Richard the Lionheart of England was sailing home from the Third Crusade when a terrible storm caught him near the little island of LOH-krum, the green island you can see just off the harbor. Richard vowed that if he survived, he'd build a church in thanks wherever he came ashore. He landed on Lokrum, but the people of Ragusa persuaded him to build it in their city instead, and he gave the money for a cathedral right here. Historians doubt it's literally true. But the people of Dubrovnik have told it for eight hundred years, and I like it. {P}

Whatever the truth, a grand Romanesque cathedral did stand here for centuries. Then came the earthquake of 1667, and it was destroyed. The Republic rebuilt it in the Baroque style, hiring an Italian architect from Urbino named Andrea Buffalini, and the new cathedral was finished in 1713. What you see is the Baroque rescue of a medieval dream.

Inside, above the main altar, is a painting of the Assumption of the Virgin, attributed to Titian, the great Venetian master, and his workshop. A genuine Renaissance treasure. {P}

The real jewel is the treasury, in a side room, with a small ticket. It holds well over a hundred reliquaries, some of the most precious in the Adriatic. And the star is Saint Blaise himself, or parts of him: his skull, in a golden reliquary shaped like a Byzantine crown, and reliquaries holding his arms and a leg, made by Dubrovnik's own goldsmiths. On his feast day, February third, they're carried through the streets in a procession that's been walked for a thousand years. {LP}

Here's a fun detail. Under the cathedral, archaeologists found the remains of older churches, going back well over a thousand years. Dubrovnik is a city of layers, and it's still being uncovered.

Your action: step inside and sit in a pew for a minute. This is a good place to rest your legs and cool off. When you're ready, walk back out and around the side of the Rector's Palace to the busy little market square with a bronze statue of a man in the middle. Press play on track six when you get there.
"""),

("06-Gundulic-Square", "Gundulić Square", "Figs, poetry and freedom", f"""
You've just walked into the heart of everyday Dubrovnik. Smell that? Citrus, lavender, and strong coffee.

Every morning, this little square turns into a market. Local farmers lay out figs, walnuts, olive oil, honey, dried herbs, lavender, and candied orange peel. If it's morning when you arrive, grab a bag of dried figs and some candied orange peel. They're a Dubrovnik tradition, and you'll be glad you did. {P}

The bronze man in the middle is Ivan GOON-doo-lich. He lived from 1589 to 1638, and he's the great poet of the Republic of Ragusa. His masterpiece is a long epic poem called Osman, about a war between the Ottoman sultan and Poland, written in Croatian at a time when many poets wrote in Latin or Italian. This statue went up in 1893. Look at the carvings around the base. They show scenes from his poem. {P}

And Gundulić gave this city its love song to liberty. In a play called Dubravka, he wrote a hymn that begins: "O beautiful, O dear, O sweet freedom." For a republic whose motto was Libertas, that hymn became part of its soul, and it's still sung at the opening of the Dubrovnik Summer Festival. Croatia even put his face on the old 50-kuna note, before the country switched to the euro. He's a literary celebrity.

One more name to remember: Marin DUR-zhich, a playwright from the 1500s who wrote comedies full of tricksters, schemers and young lovers. You can feel a bit of his spirit here, in the haggling and the gossip of the market. Dubrovnik loved its comedy as much as its saints. {LP}

Your action: this is a great time for a snack. Pick up something from a stall, or get a coffee at one of the cafés around the edge. Then look for the grand staircase at the far side of the square, rising up like a theater set. That's our last stop, the Jesuit Stairs. Press play on track seven when you reach the foot of the stairs.
"""),

("07-Jesuit-Stairs", "The Jesuit Stairs and Church of St. Ignatius", "A little piece of Rome", f"""
Take a look at the sweep of these steps. You've just finished a wonderful walk, and this is the grand finale.

These are the Jesuit Stairs, built in 1738. And Kim, Dave, you'll recognize the idea. {P} You were just in Rome. The architect, Pietro Passalacqua, modeled them on the Spanish Steps. Just like Rome, they curve in graceful flights and swing up to a church at the top. Dubrovnik wanted a little piece of Rome, and it built one.

At the top is the Church of Saint Ignatius, the Jesuit church, finished in the 1720s and modeled on the Gesù, the great Jesuit mother church in Rome. Inside, the walls behind the altar are covered with frescoes of the life of Saint Ignatius of Loyola, painted by a Spanish Jesuit artist, Gaetano Garcia. If the doors are open, it's worth the climb. {P} It's like stepping into Rome in miniature.

Next to the church was the Jesuit college. And here's a name you should know: ROO-jer BOSH-ko-vich. He was born in Dubrovnik in 1711 and studied right here as a boy. He became one of the great scientists of the eighteenth century: a physicist, astronomer, mathematician, and a Jesuit priest. His ideas about the forces inside matter helped shape how later scientists, like Michael Faraday, thought about atoms. The little square at the top of the stairs is named for him. {P} So when you stand up there, you're standing at the boyhood school of a man who helped write the physics books.

One pop culture moment: TV fans know these steps from Game of Thrones, where Cersei Lannister made her famous walk of shame down them. Whatever you think of the show, the stairs make a perfect stage. {LP}

Climb them slowly. They're gentle and wide. When you reach the top, turn around and look back. You'll see the rooftops, the cathedral dome, and the sea beyond.

Kim and Dave, that's the walk. You've seen a pharmacy that's been open since 1317, a customs house that survived an earthquake, a saint who saved a city, a ruler who couldn't leave the house, a fortress built to be taken back, and a staircase borrowed from Rome. And you did it your way, at your own pace, in one of the most beautiful cities in the world.

Your action: before you head back, find a spot with a view, take a photo together, and send it to the family. When you're ready, walk down the stairs, cross the market square, and follow the Stradun back toward Pee-leh Gate. Keep an eye on your all-aboard time. Have a wonderful rest of your cruise. {P} And get the gelato. You've earned it.
"""),
]

